From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0333-frizti-03-cpu-writes-to-list-pages-count-at-once.patch

Source: 0333-frizti-03-cpu-writes-to-list-pages-count-at-once.patch
buffer_cache: a CPU write to a GPU-written list page counts at once (off by default)

Core patch. Paths are the submodule's. Applies on core16 alone.

Source: sylvieistaken/selevet-eglantine a3644b1b3e (Performance build 7), GPL-3.0-or-later. Not in
Lichii or Frizti. Zortos's Selevet audit is older than that build.

EDEN_NX2_LIST_CPU_SYNC=1. A CPU write to a page the GPU wrote is collected until the next fence. If
the page holds command lists, the front meanwhile takes the host copy as current and runs its old
lists, a whole earlier frame. Selevet reports old frames flashing between new ones in Ocarina, fixed
by this, on one RTX 3050 machine. Now the write marks the copy stale at once. The list then goes the
flush way, which first applies the collected writes, or the download would put the old lists back.

Goes with nemyu-02 (EDEN_NX2_HELD_WRITES), it does not replace it: that one gets the bytes to the GPU
before a list, this one stops the front reading an old copy of the list itself.

Limit, as in the source: OnCPUWrite runs only when a core moves to another page, so only the first
write of a run on a page counts at once. Nothing shows the console has these flashes at all.

Changed from the source: off by default; there it is on, with =0 to leave it. The flush here is the
one core16 has, without Selevet's page batch.

Runs on the PS5: same core path. No log says how often the console's Ocarina reaches it.
Hang: no new kind of wait. Such lists take the drain and flush core16 already has for a page's first
list, possibly more often, so it can cost time. Running stale lists is the worse failure: stale draw
arguments lost the device in Fast Fusion.
Pool memory: none.
Checked: PS5-target syntax compile, dma_pusher and the buffer cache. Not run.

